# BinderHaven
# 15_CARD_EXPERIENCE

Version: 1.0
Status: Draft
Project: BinderHaven
Company: Poole Labs

---

# Purpose

The Card Experience represents the final level of interaction within BinderHaven.

Every card is more than an inventory record.

It is a collectible.

The experience should celebrate the artwork, history, and significance of each card while providing collectors with meaningful information.

---

# Experience Philosophy

The card is the hero.

Statistics support the card.

They never compete with it.

The collector should always see the card before they see its metadata.

BinderHaven exists to celebrate collecting.

The Card Experience should reflect that philosophy.

---

# Emotional Objectives

When opening a card, collectors should feel:

• Appreciation

• Curiosity

• Pride

• Excitement

• Satisfaction

The experience should encourage collectors to spend time viewing their cards rather than simply extracting information.

---

# Transition Philosophy

Opening a card should feel like lifting it from the binder.

The selected card becomes the visual focus while the surrounding interface gracefully recedes.

The transition should feel connected to the Page Experience.

Collectors should never feel as though they are navigating to an unrelated screen.

---

# Card Presentation

The card should occupy the majority of the available space.

The artwork should become the primary visual element.

Characteristics:

• Large display

• High-resolution imagery

• Accurate colors

• Correct proportions

• Minimal distractions

The collector's attention should immediately focus on the artwork.

---

# Information Hierarchy

Information should appear in the following order:

1. Card

2. Card Name

3. Set Information

4. Card Number

5. Rarity

6. Type

7. Ownership Status

8. Market Information

Supporting details should remain visually secondary.

---

# Market Information

Pricing exists to support collectors.

It should never dominate the experience.

Examples include:

• Current Market Value

• Last Updated

• Price Trend

Future versions may support additional pricing providers.

Market information should remain contextual.

BinderHaven is not a stock portfolio.

---

# Ownership

Ownership information should clearly communicate the collector's relationship with the card.

Examples:

• Owned

• Missing

• Wishlist

• Duplicate

• Graded

• Favorite

Status should remain informative without overwhelming the interface.

---

# Artwork First

Artwork is central to the collecting experience.

Whenever possible, the artwork should receive more visual emphasis than statistics.

Collectors should feel encouraged to appreciate the illustration as much as the card itself.

---

# Card Actions

Common actions may include:

• View Details

• Mark Owned

• Mark Missing

• Add Notes

• Add to Wishlist

• View Market Information

• Share

Actions should remain easily accessible without interrupting appreciation of the card.

---

# Card Details

Supporting information may include:

• Artist

• Set

• Release Date

• Language

• Finish

• Variants

• Product Source

• Collection Notes

Information should remain organized and easy to browse.

---

# Motion Philosophy

Cards should feel lightweight yet tangible.

Animations should communicate care.

Possible interactions include:

• Gentle lift

• Soft shadow

• Smooth transitions

• Subtle parallax

Motion should enhance realism without becoming distracting.

---

# Session Persistence

When returning from Card Detail, collectors should return to:

• The same binder

• The same page

• The same scroll position

The transition should feel seamless.

---

# Accessibility

Artwork should remain viewable at larger sizes.

Typography should remain readable.

Interactive controls should remain accessible across supported devices.

Reduced Motion settings should provide simplified transitions.

---

# Future Enhancements

Future releases may introduce:

• Grading information

• Scan comparison

• AI-assisted card identification

• Collection history

• Price history

• Personal notes

• Card achievements

• Alternate artwork viewer

These additions should extend the experience without reducing clarity.

---

# Success Criteria

A successful Card Experience should:

• Celebrate the artwork

• Reinforce ownership

• Present information clearly

• Feel connected to the binder

• Encourage exploration

• Scale naturally as features expand

If collectors open a card simply to admire it—not because they need information—the design has succeeded.